{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>But he will not listen.</b> It is difficult: after it says ״ואני אקשה״ (and I will harden), what room is there to say ״ולא ישמע״ (and he will not listen)? One must further know what this new ״putting of My hand״ is, that is not included among the signs and wonders mentioned above regarding the hardening of his heart. There is further a fine point to examine in the verse." "<b>Indeed,</b> the intent of the verse is: at first He said <b>ואני אקשה</b> (and I will harden) etc., and ״I will multiply,״ and He further informs that after He multiplies wonders, Pharaoh the wicked will add transgression upon his sin, and will not wish to hear the message from you any longer when I send you to him. And so it was, as it is written (Exodus 10:28): ״אל תוסף ראות פני״ (Do not see my face again) etc. And HaShem informs Moses that when he does so, then is his boundary, for immediately and at once HaShem will put His hand — meaning, the plague of the firstborn — in the manner of His saying (1 Chronicles 21:16): ״וחרבו שלופה בידו״ (and his sword drawn in his hand)." "And He said in the modified expression to Him <b>את ידי</b> (et My hand), to say that this plague HaShem will perform with His own hand literally, as it were, as they said of blessed memory (Mekhilta Bo): ״I, and not an angel, and not a seraph, and not an emissary, but the Holy One blessed be He in His glory״ etc., and through the medium of this plague, immediately and at once, I will bring out My hosts. And He also alluded to what He did to their gods — He executed great judgments upon them in the plague of the firstborn, as is known." "<b>And with this</b> my mind is satisfied regarding Moses' statement there, when Pharaoh said to him ״אל תוסף״ (Do not add) etc., he answered him ״כן דברת לא אוסיף״ (As you have spoken, I will not add) etc. And it is difficult concerning Moses our master: on what did he rely to say ״לא אוסיף״ (I will not add) etc., and if our Elohim were to say to him ״Go to Pharaoh״ another time, what would he answer? But according to what we have explained, his reason is praiseworthy, for HaShem had told him that when he reaches the degree of refusal to hear their words, that is the end of His decree to stretch out HaShem's hand etc. and to bring them out. Therefore when he said to him ״אל תוסף״ etc., behold — the prey of hearing [is won], that he would not hear anything more from them, small or great, and he even forbade himself from seeing their faces. Therefore immediately he said to him ״כן דברת לא אוסיף״ etc., but rather ״your ear will yet hear a thing now״ etc. And this matter of the plague of the firstborn HaShem had already informed him, as I explained in parashat Shemot at the verse (Exodus 4:22) ״כה אמר ה' בני בכורי ישראל״ — he derived it from there." "<b>And I will bring out My hosts, My people</b> etc. One must know who these hosts are, and if they are Israel, it should have said ״את צבאות עמי בני ישראל״ (the hosts of My people, the children of Israel). Perhaps it is that HaShem intended to record in the Torah the surpassing excellence of Israel toward Him, for He has no hosts uniquely designated for Him like Israel, and therefore if He had said ״את צבאות עמי״ etc., there would be room to say that HaShem has many hosts of hosts and this is one of them. Therefore He said <b>את צבאותי</b> (et My hosts) without specification, to say: the recorded ones, and there is no need to say ״the host of so-and-so,״ for when you say ״the hosts of HaShem,״ He is the one uniquely designated, and afterward He said who this is and which it is — et <b>My people, the children of Israel.</b> And the verse may be explained by the rule of general and particular — there is nothing in the general, ״My hosts,״ except for the particular, which is the children of Israel, and from now on none will be called by the designation ״the hosts of HaShem״ except Israel, who are exalted and elevated."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, 